00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:06.440
That's pretty cool.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:07.840
 A lot of people--

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.690
a lot of our fans, let's say,
go see the movie more than once.

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.910
And so this is
like a way to go--

00:05:13.910 --> 00:05:16.030
you saw it in 2D
that first night.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.460
But you go back the second night
or the third night or whatever.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:19.260
And you see it in 3D.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.060
And you go, oh, my god.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:21.080
It's like seeing a new movie.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.090
 One of the things
that I want to dig into

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.570
is, like, just the
biology of it all.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.450
How does that affect the work
that you and your team do?

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:33.480
 Well, if you think about it,
we experience the world in 3D

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.280
all the time, right?

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.230
With our two eyes
taking the world in,

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:38.510
and our brain is taking
those two separate images

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.340
and fusing them
and giving you an--

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.980
what we call depth perception.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.520
When you go to the movies, for
many years, it's been flat.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.260
Which, in a weird
way, is what people

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.690
have come to think is normal.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.100
3D in the cinema, though, is
a little bit different than 3D

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.660
in real life, in
that it's actually

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.190
a magic trick of sorts.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:57.650
But we're going to trick your
brain into thinking that things

00:05:57.650 --> 00:05:59.660
have dimension and depth.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.730
But at the end of the day,
you put those glasses on.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:04.540
And hopefully, it
feels like Spider-Man

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:05.540
is right there with you.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.850
 You know, in the trailer
for Spider-Man, Far From Home,

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:11.210
we saw this water creature.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:15.120
We saw a big fire monster
sort of destroying the bridge.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.960
And, like, all these things
that Spider-Man is up against.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:21.650
Of all the fun elements
in play in Spider-Man,

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:26.420
Far From Home, I would imagine
the most is Spider-Man himself.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.680
Because he's Spider-Man.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.480
He's the greatest
superhero of all time.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:33.470
EVAN JACOBS: Well, what's cool
about him is he does a lot more

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:35.120
flying around in this movie.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.150
So there's a lot of
fun opportunities.

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:40.420
Anytime we can get
him airborne and off--

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:43.340
you know, or shooting webs or
whatever, there's a lot of--

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:45.290
those are always great, because
it can bring the audience

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:46.495
on the ride with you.

00:06:46.495 --> 00:06:48.095
 I want to talk a little
bit about rotoscoping.

00:06:48.095 --> 00:06:48.895
 Yeah.

00:06:48.895 --> 00:06:50.480
It's so much fun.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:54.690
 What is rotoscoping, in terms
of the stereoscopic experience

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.080
that you're working on?

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:00.650
 The basic idea is tracing
photograph images, right?

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:04.520
If you think about what 3D
is in the context of a movie,

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.290
you sort of start with
this flat image that would

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:07.610
be sitting right on the screen.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.140
And then you want
some of the things

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:12.020
to be identified as
distant background,

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:14.420
some in the mid-ground,
some in the foreground.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:18.156
And to do that with a flat, 2D
image that you photographed,

00:07:18.156 --> 00:07:19.580
you got to retrace
the whole thing.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.220
So you trace every
single thing in the frame

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.680
that you want to have
on a different layer.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:26.210
That's how you separate
these people, right?

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:27.920
That's the first
step of this process.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.500
And that's what we
call rotoscoping.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:32.720
There's a procedural process
that would maybe take the edges

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.520
and stretch them out, but
that doesn't look very good.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.250
So pretty quickly,
you've got to go in

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:38.930
and hand paint all that stuff.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.360
 So you're rotoscoping
the entire film?

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.290
 Every single frame.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:47.480
 Every-- I'm trying
to process, like,

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:51.590
the amount of people that you
need, the manpower, the time.

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.820
And the attention to
detail, because it's not a--

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.130
you know, the way
you describe it,

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.020
it doesn't seem like
something-- just

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:59.380
someone puts their headphones
on and blindly does it.

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:00.740
 No, it just--

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:01.730
it's a crazy process.

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:04.370
But you know, one of the things
that's really fun about it

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.410
is when you get a filmmaker
in at the end of the thing,

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:08.930
and you've done a sequence,
and you've taken their film

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:12.350
and presenting it back to
them in a completely new way.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:16.310
And then they react, you
know, as if-- as if they've

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:17.707
never seen the movie.

00:08:17.707 --> 00:08:18.540
 That's so cool, though.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.115
 That's just the
craziest thing.

00:08:20.115 --> 00:08:22.310
 Yeah, it's really fun.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.040
One of the things that I think
is, you know-- differentiates

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:27.320
the Marvel films from some
of the other films that

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.300
are out there in the marketplace
that are released in 3D

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:32.750
is that we're really
involved all the way through.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:34.780
 I heard a term
as we were starting

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.470
to put this together, something
called a depth script.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:38.270
 Yeah.

00:08:38.270 --> 00:08:39.409
 What is the depth script?

00:08:39.409 --> 00:08:41.580
And how does that play
into this whole process?

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.940
 If you think about it, 3D can
be just like a musical score

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:50.480
or just about any other part of
a film, in that you essentially

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:51.470
have a depth script.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:53.390
And you sort of can be,
like, I can make it very

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:55.010
deep in this section,
because it's going to be

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:56.180
a very exciting 3D moment.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:59.480
And then I can give
the audience a break,

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:01.350
and, you know, come
down a little bit.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.680
And then I go back up
again for something else.

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:06.740
And that kind of ebb
and flow of the process

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:09.230
is a creative
control that we have,

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:10.390
so that people experience it.

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:13.340
So if you think about it, just
in the musical context, if you

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:15.080
just kept it loud all
the time, you wouldn't

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:16.280
really know that it was loud.

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:17.080
You know?

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:18.880
You're not always doing
the cool 3D just to do it.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:19.680
RYAN PENAGOS: Yeah.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.710
 You're doing it if it affects
the-- if it helps the movie.

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:22.190
 Yeah.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:23.750
Evan, I'm super excited
to see this movie.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.960
You have completely sold me
here, this whole conversation.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.380
But for our fans who, you
know, have seen it once,

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.330
maybe haven't seen
it in 3D yet, why

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:34.370
do they have to see it in 3D?

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:36.080
 It's a completely
different experience in 2D.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:38.600
So the elementals
are incredible.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:42.060
There's some other stuff I
can't talk about that's amazing.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:42.920
 What?

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:44.840
 And really, really
fun and disorienting

00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.270
and cool and exciting.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:48.333
And it's going to get--

00:09:48.333 --> 00:09:49.500
we're going to take
them on a ride.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.840
So it's definitely--

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:53.070
I'm very proud of this movie.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:56.160
And I'm proud of the
work that we did.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.620
There's some of the films
that we work on really

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.980
lend themselves to the format.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:03.180
And this is one of those films.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:04.890
Avoiding spoilers, I can't
tell you much about it,

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:05.850
but you gotta go see it.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:06.650
 I'm telling you.

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:07.680
The cameras are off.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:08.760
You can tell me everything.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:09.560
 All right.

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:10.710
 OK.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.105
There you have it, everybody.

00:10:12.105 --> 00:10:13.510
Evan, thank you so much.

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:17.830
I am a full convert
into the 3D experience.

00:10:17.830 --> 00:10:18.630
I need it.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:19.660
I want it.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.240
I hope you guys want it, too.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.430
Spider-Man, Far From
Home is in theaters now.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:25.930
Be sure to see it in 3D.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:27.390
 Oh, so good.

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:30.570
I love Spider-Man in 3D,
especially because Spider-Man--

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:32.370
by virtue of being
Spider-Man-- is always

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:33.450
swinging through the buildings.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:34.360
And that's so cool in 3D.

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:35.160
 Yeah.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.797
And I got to say, all the visual
effects in every single frame,

00:10:37.797 --> 00:10:39.030
just going through
every single frame

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.480
and doing all these minor
adjustments and minor tweaks,

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.310
hey, thank you to
that VFX team--

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:44.110
 Oh, my god.

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.410
 --for making
Spider-Man look so good.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:46.690
 Someone get these
people coffee.

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:47.490
Oh, my goodness.

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:48.290
That's a lot of work.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:49.110
You know, I'm going to--

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:51.120
I think I'm going to take
a European vacation myself

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:51.610
like Spidey.

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:52.777
I'm just not going
to fight anyone.

00:10:52.777 --> 00:10:53.640
Don't try to make me.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:54.450
It's my vacation.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:55.510
 I'm looking for the fights.

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:56.875
- Oh!
- I want to fight some baddies.

00:10:56.875 --> 00:10:57.920
- All right.
- Yeah.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:10:58.410
Well, listen.

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:00.060
Hey, tell us where in
the Marvel universe

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.950
you think would make
the best vacation spot.

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:03.870
And use the hashtag
#EarthsMightiestShow.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:04.860
 Oh, and this is cool.

